23:26.380 --> 23:30.060
overflow and the subsequent reversal of these start and end

23:30.060 --> 23:33.860
values for range checking, this check that is designed to

23:33.860 --> 23:38.520
guarantee that this range is not secure returns true even though

23:38.520 --> 23:43.200
it clearly should be returning false. Now the result of this

23:43.200 --> 23:47.700
bug is that we can craft an SMC request that exploits this

23:47.700 --> 23:52.500
integer overflow and causes QSEE to write a response header to

23:52.500 --> 23:56.900
arbitrary secure memory. And this is clearly a serious

23:56.900 --> 23:59.740
vulnerability that can result in compromise. And the goal from

23:59.740 --> 24:02.980
this point forward is to achieve arbitrary secure code

24:02.980 --> 24:08.320
execution on these devices. The first thing to look at is

24:08.320 --> 24:10.820
clearly what gets written to a response header. What kind of

24:10.820 --> 24:14.820
data can we cause QSEE to write to arbitrary secure memory. And

24:14.820 --> 24:19.820
turns out that the response header is hard coded in all cases. It

24:19.820 --> 24:23.620
writes the length as 12 which is just the size of the response

24:23.620 --> 24:28.060
header. The buffer offset is 12 and then this is complete is set

24:28.060 --> 24:31.060
to one. So the final result is that we can write these three

24:31.060 --> 24:38.480
words 12, 12, 1 to arbitrary secure memory. Exploiting this is

24:38.480 --> 24:42.020
somewhat of a challenge but a few things make it easier. The

24:42.020 --> 24:45.660
first of these is the memory layout of QSE is almost entirely

24:45.660 --> 24:49.520
known to the attacker. Because the trust zone image sits on

24:49.520 --> 24:52.360
disk and we're assuming we're running as the kernel, we can

24:52.360 --> 24:55.760
read that image from disk and because it is loaded at a known

24:55.760 --> 25:00.380
constant physical address in memory, we essentially know

25:00.380 --> 25:03.400
every, we know the entire layout of the trust zone image in

25:03.400 --> 25:06.340
memory even though we can't explicitly read the secure

25:06.340 --> 25:10.340
memory where it resides. Next, it's helpful that most of RAM

25:10.340 --> 25:13.640
is actually non-secure memory so we have a somewhat large sort of

25:13.640 --> 25:16.480
scratch pad to work with when we're trying to do things in

25:16.480 --> 25:20.420
memory. Now the sort of classic approach that you would take if

25:20.420 --> 25:24.280
you were approaching this like a traditional kernel exploit maybe

25:24.280 --> 25:27.100
to find some sort of function pointer that lives in secure

25:27.100 --> 25:31.060
memory and then use our write primitive to overwrite some of

25:31.060 --> 25:34.740
the bytes of that function pointer so that it now points to

25:34.740 --> 25:37.540
an address residing in non-secure memory. Then we

25:37.540 --> 25:40.920
could put a payload at that non secure address and cause that

25:40.920 --> 25:44.280
function pointer to be invoked by trust zone and then the result

25:44.280 --> 25:46.880
would be trust zone running our non secure payload and that would

25:46.880 --> 25:52.780
be victory. Unfortunately, this doesn't actually appear to work.

25:52.780 --> 25:56.500
So I suspect that QSEE actually has some sort of mechanism in

25:56.500 --> 25:59.200
place that prevents trust zone from executing code from non

25:59.200 --> 26:02.700
secure pages. Sort of analogously to how user land

26:02.700 --> 26:08.700
applications have NX or DEP or more recently the Linux kernel

26:08.700 --> 26:15.640
on ARM or X86 have PXN and SMEP respectively. And I suspect it

26:15.640 --> 26:18.540
may have to do with the XPU which is memory management

26:18.540 --> 26:21.880
component that handles marking regions as secure. But this is

26:21.880 --> 26:25.720
an undocumented black hole. It's very difficult to understand

26:25.720 --> 26:28.620
what's going on here and I don't have the capabilities to look

26:28.620 --> 26:30.660
at the hardware close enough to actually understand what's

26:30.660 --> 26:34.260
going on. So this is the part of the talk where I coyly ask if

26:34.260 --> 26:37.000
there are any Qualcomm or ARM employees in the audience who

26:37.000 --> 26:40.700
want to answer this question as to whether this is the case but

26:40.700 --> 26:42.740
I'll let them off the hook because I assume they can't

26:42.740 --> 26:48.880
say. So we need to take another approach to actually

26:48.880 --> 26:51.080
gaining arbitrary code execution because it's clear

26:51.080 --> 26:53.480
that at least from experimentation it's not

26:53.480 --> 26:55.980
going to work. We're not going to be able to cause trust zone

26:55.980 --> 26:59.160
to execute non secure code. We need to find a way to cause

26:59.160 --> 27:02.660
trust zone to execute secure code in a way that allows us to

27:02.660 --> 27:08.660
win. Now the fact that this is a 12-byte memory write with

27:08.660 --> 27:12.040
where we don't control the contents makes exploitation

27:12.040 --> 27:15.440
somewhat difficult. So the first thing that's a little bit

27:15.440 --> 27:18.380
tricky is that because it's a 12-byte write, if we want to

27:18.380 --> 27:21.620
overwrite one word in memory, then we're necessarily

27:21.620 --> 27:25.320
clobbering two adjacent words to that which could potentially

27:25.320 --> 27:29.860
lead to instability depending on what the target is. And then

27:29.860 --> 27:33.460
additionally, probably more importantly, we have very minimal

27:33.460 --> 27:36.500
options in terms of what we can do because we have this very

27:36.500 --> 27:40.140
limited vocabulary of what we can cause to be written in secure

27:40.140 --> 27:45.340
memory. We only have zeros, a 12, and a 1. That's fairly

27:45.340 --> 27:50.280
limited. So the challenge here is actually coming up with a way

27:50.280 --> 27:53.820
of using this first exploit primitive to build a more

27:53.820 --> 28:00.820
flexible one that allows us to achieve our goals. Now, revisiting these secure memory

28:02.820 --> 28:08.820
protection functions, so we recall that QSE checks the status of memory regions by iterating

28:10.900 --> 28:17.820
over this linked list which has hard coded regions and each of these regions is encapsulated

28:17.820 --> 28:23.820
by a structure that is similar to this. This is just what I reverse engineered. Each region

28:23.820 --> 28:27.820
structure has an ID which is just an incremental identifier.

28:27.820 --> 28:30.660
Flags that indicate the actual protection attributes of that

28:30.660 --> 28:36.500
region and then the start and end of these regions. So what I

28:36.500 --> 28:40.100
came up with was that we can actually use our 12 byte right

28:40.100 --> 28:44.340
to overwrite the flags start and end addresses of the region

28:44.340 --> 28:48.720
entry corresponding to the QSEE image. So the result of this

28:48.720 --> 28:53.820
is anytime QSEE goes to check the safety of a pointer provided

28:53.820 --> 28:57.460
the kernel to guarantee that it's safe for it to assume that it's

28:57.460 --> 28:59.860
non-secure and that it can read from it and write to it without

28:59.860 --> 29:04.500
consequence. If that pointer resides in the address space

29:04.500 --> 29:08.000
reserved for the QSE image it's going to say it's fine. It's

29:08.000 --> 29:13.400
safe. And at this point this gives us the ability to write to

29:13.400 --> 29:17.580
arbitrary secure memory with any value that QSE writes as output

29:17.580 --> 29:22.480
at any point. Here we can basically choose a new write

29:22.480 --> 29:26.020
primitive. We've expanded our vocabulary for what we're able to

29:26.020 --> 29:29.520
write to secure memory. So at this point I enumerated all of the

29:29.520 --> 29:33.320
SMC handlers, eliminated those that don't write any output at

29:33.320 --> 29:37.900
any point. And now we've got a list of things that we can write

29:37.900 --> 29:41.100
to arbitrary secure memory. And we can, depending on how we

29:41.100 --> 29:44.100
design the rest of the exploit, just pick the one that suits the

29:44.100 --> 29:48.180
task at hand. And now the only missing piece here is actually

29:48.180 --> 29:51.380
finding a way to win. You know, we have a more flexible write

29:51.380 --> 29:57.780
primitive but how do we actually get code execution? Well,

29:57.780 --> 30:02.520
revisiting how QSE actually handles SMC instructions. So

30:02.520 --> 30:06.620
when an SMC instruction is issued, QSE will parse the

30:06.620 --> 30:11.240
command structure and it will iterate over a table with these

30:11.240 --> 30:15.280
variable length entries where each entry corresponds to an

30:15.280 --> 30:20.180
SMC handler, sort of like a system call handler, where each

30:20.180 --> 30:24.180
entry contains an SMC number which is just the identifier of

30:24.180 --> 30:28.980
the SMC request. A pointer to a string describing the handler

30:28.980 --> 30:31.620
which was incredibly useful when trying to figure out what each

30:31.620 --> 30:36.120
of these handlers actually did. Various flags. And then a

30:36.120 --> 30:39.540
function pointer to the actual function that gets invoked by

30:39.540 --> 30:44.580
QSE when this SMC request matching this ID is issued. A

30:44.580 --> 30:48.140
number of arguments field and then a variable length array

30:48.140 --> 30:51.180
whose length depends on this number of arguments field because

30:51.180 --> 30:56.840
this variable length array is actually contains one entry for

30:56.840 --> 30:59.460
each argument and each entry corresponds to the length of

30:59.460 --> 31:06.460
each argument. So I ended up using the ability to write

31:08.600 --> 31:14.100
essentially arbitrary data to arbitrary QSE memory to create

31:14.100 --> 31:18.240
what I call an SMC table extension attack. So I actually use

31:18.240 --> 31:22.500
arbitrary secure memory to modify the number of arguments field

31:22.500 --> 31:28.180
in one of the SMC tables. Table entries rather. Now what I did

31:28.180 --> 31:32.660
was I actually expanded the size of one of these SMC entries so

31:32.660 --> 31:37.460
that it's particularly massive and that when QSE is iterating

31:37.460 --> 31:42.160
through this table and tries to find the location of the next

31:42.160 --> 31:47.240
SMC entry it will actually jump way into non secure attacker

31:47.240 --> 31:52.000
controlled memory. At which point we can put a fake entry

31:52.000 --> 31:56.840
there and at this point we control an SMC handler. We can

31:56.840 --> 32:01.780
make up an SMC ID, point the function pointer anywhere we want

32:01.780 --> 32:08.020
in secure memory and craft any sort of arbitrary arguments and

32:08.020 --> 32:12.200
basically call any function in trust zone at whim with any

32:12.200 --> 32:15.600
arguments we want which in my opinion is a fairly elegant way

32:15.600 --> 32:21.460
to gain total control of trust zone. At this point we have

32:21.460 --> 32:26.880
arbitrary trust zone code execution and we have one. One

32:26.880 --> 32:29.240
of the fun things you can do is actually find the mem copy

32:29.240 --> 32:32.940
implementation in the trust zone kernel and actually just

32:32.940 --> 32:36.340
invoke that to copy all of secure memory to a non secure

32:36.340 --> 32:42.040
buffer. So this would allow you to steal any DRM or secure key

32:42.040 --> 32:45.480
storage related stuff. Any sensitive data stored in secure

32:45.480 --> 32:49.160
memory now becomes available to the attacker. You could disable

32:49.160 --> 32:51.780
TEMA. You could disable any piece of security related

32:51.780 --> 32:55.020
functionality and trust zone. You have full control. And then

32:55.020 --> 32:59.160
perhaps the flashiest thing you can do is invoke OEM specific

32:59.160 --> 33:01.600
functionality to do things like permanently unlock a boot

33:01.600 --> 33:06.340
loader of a device. So that's what I'm going to do right now.

33:06.340 --> 33:12.340
So I have on stage a Verizon branded Moto X. So the way that

33:12.340 --> 33:18.340
Motorola implements secure boot is by featuring a manufacturer

33:18.340 --> 33:24.340
sponsored mechanism to unlock the boot loader which would allow

33:24.340 --> 33:27.340
users to install custom operating systems and so forth.

33:27.340 --> 33:32.340
And the way they do that is the user actually sends Motorola a

33:32.340 --> 33:36.820
request token that they got from their device. If that token

33:36.820 --> 33:40.480
matches a device on an approved carrier, then Motorola will send

33:40.480 --> 33:43.460
a token in response that the user can then issue to the

33:43.460 --> 33:46.160
boot loader and the boot loader will make a request of trust

33:46.160 --> 33:52.060
zone to unlock permanently by blowing a QFuse. Now, for

33:52.060 --> 33:57.060
Verizon and AT&T, they just won't give you a response token

33:57.060 --> 33:59.900
back, so there's no way to unlock your boot loader, except

33:59.900 --> 34:04.580
if you have a single vulnerability you can actually do it yourself by just invoking the

34:04.580 --> 34:10.580
functionality that they would do if you gave them the response token. So here I've got

34:10.580 --> 34:16.580
we've got a Verizon Moto X running Android 4.4. As you can see it is not unlocked and the

34:16.580 --> 34:22.580
running Android 4.4. As you can see it is not unlocked. And the

34:24.820 --> 34:30.820
warranty is still present. So I need to continue booting. This

34:30.820 --> 34:35.700
will take just a moment. So this is an incredibly risky demo

34:35.700 --> 34:38.960
because first of all it's live which is always a bad idea but

34:38.960 --> 34:43.140
additionally you can only do this once to a phone because

34:43.140 --> 34:48.140
you're permanently blowing a fuse. So I obviously have never tested it on this phone. So who knows

34:48.140 --> 34:52.800
what's going to happen. I think it's the second riskiest thing I did in this talk. The first

34:52.800 --> 34:59.800
was using open office to make my slides. Okay. So just using ADB, we have a shell. I'm going

35:01.900 --> 35:07.900
to use an implementation of the recent Linux vulnerability to escalate privileges to root.

35:07.900 --> 35:14.900
and then I'm going to cross my fingers and hope that this works. Well, it didn't break.

35:14.900 --> 35:20.900
Well, it didn't break. And we're going to pull up the

35:20.900 --> 35:24.900
Well, it didn't break. And we're going to pull up the

35:37.040 --> 35:39.660
boot order variables again and we can see that the device is now

35:39.660 --> 35:49.660
unlocked. So at this point you can actually use fast boot

35:49.660 --> 35:53.500
use fast boot which is built into the boot loader to flash any

35:53.500 --> 35:57.000
software you would like on this phone. So as far as I know this

35:57.000 --> 36:00.580
is the only or one of the only Verizon Moto X's in the world

36:00.580 --> 36:06.040
that's unlocked. So a few lessons can be learned from this

36:06.040 --> 36:09.340
research. One of the most practical ones is that analysis

36:09.340 --> 36:13.820
and exploitation of trust zone was made much easier due to the

36:13.820 --> 36:16.680
fact that there's no sort of protection or encryption of the

36:16.680 --> 36:22.760
trust zone image itself. You can compare this to iOS where trust zone is stored encrypted on disk

36:22.760 --> 36:30.760
and as a result without either a leak of firmware from Apple or exploiting trust zone itself

36:30.760 --> 36:35.260
which is basically impossible if you've never had a trust zone image to analyze in the first

36:35.260 --> 36:40.380
place. You need an iBoot vulnerability to actually gain access to a decrypted trust zone

36:40.380 --> 36:48.380
image to analyze in the first place. Next, parsing of complex data structures, in this case these

36:48.380 --> 36:54.380
command buffers seem like a fairly obvious point of failure. So as a vulnerability researcher

36:54.380 --> 37:00.380
places like this are really good targets for scrutiny. The next thing is as a researcher

37:00.380 --> 37:06.380
it's important to learn to develop a spider sense and learn how to trust your gut. If it looks

37:06.380 --> 37:12.260
sketchy it very likely is. In this case the minute I saw in the assembly code this reversal of

37:12.260 --> 37:16.920
arguments in the case of an erroneous call that seemed to yell out at me as something that was

37:16.920 --> 37:21.260
potentially bad even though I didn't know immediately what that would be and it turned

37:21.260 --> 37:28.260
out to be a very critical vulnerability. Next just looking at for example boot order design

37:28.260 --> 37:33.140
single points of failure are a bad idea. When you look at Samsung's implementation of secure

37:33.140 --> 37:35.880
boot, for example, there's no single point of failure, there's

37:35.880 --> 37:39.440
no off switch that allows you to exploit something and unlock

37:39.440 --> 37:43.220
the boot loader. In contrast to Motorola, which made a design

37:43.220 --> 37:45.680
decision that makes it easier for them because now they don't

37:45.680 --> 37:48.820
have to ship different software or hardware for different

37:48.820 --> 37:53.160
carriers, but they also included a mechanism by which any single

37:53.160 --> 37:56.400
trust zone vulnerability will result in a permanent unlock of

37:56.400 --> 37:59.700
Motorola boot loader. Which is great if you're like me and you

37:59.700 --> 38:03.040
like installing stuff on your phone but not so good for Motorola's

38:03.040 --> 38:09.040
customers. Next, I think the basic idea and design of trust zone

38:09.040 --> 38:15.580
is excellent. Having hardware enforced security boundaries and

38:15.580 --> 38:19.380
a minimal core operating system component that is considered

38:19.380 --> 38:25.500
trusted is a great idea. But unfortunately what I think we've

38:25.500 --> 38:29.500
been seeing here is that so many vendors are so jazzed about

38:29.500 --> 38:34.000
trust zone that there has been so much feature bloat that the

38:34.000 --> 38:37.580
attack surface in trust zone itself has widened substantially

38:37.580 --> 38:40.080
and that sort of eliminates one of the big advantages of having

38:40.080 --> 38:43.680
a system like trust zone which is designed to be small. And then

38:43.680 --> 38:46.560
finally I think everyone in here already knew this but the best

38:46.560 --> 38:49.020
marketing team in the world will not change the fact that

38:49.020 --> 38:51.620
software is written by humans and humans occasionally make

38:51.620 --> 38:54.860
mistakes. So despite the fact that something may be billed as

38:54.860 --> 38:59.360
100% secure. And no joke I have actually seen that in media

38:59.360 --> 39:03.200
reports about trust zone. That does not mean that it's not

39:03.200 --> 39:05.440
worthy of attention and scrutiny and there aren't bugs

39:05.440 --> 39:11.440
there. That's it. So I had a contact slide. But that's all

39:11.440 --> 39:14.940
right. That's the end of my talk. Thank you.

39:24.860 --> 39:54.840
Thank you.

